fixpp package provenance
========================

This file identifies the exact build that produced the artifact it ships in.
It exists because the artifact directory deliberately outlives the build-tree
deletion cycle, so packages from different configurations and different source
states accumulate side by side (084 FR-021/FR-021a).

product              : fixpp
version              : @FIXPP_STAMP_VERSION@
platform             : @FIXPP_STAMP_PLATFORM@
toolchain            : @FIXPP_STAMP_COMPILER_ID@ @FIXPP_STAMP_COMPILER_VERSION@
configuration        : @FIXPP_STAMP_CONFIG@
telemetry            : @FIXPP_PACKAGE_TELEMETRY@
source-revision      : @FIXPP_PACKAGE_REVISION@
source-worktree      : @FIXPP_PACKAGE_WORKTREE@

Field notes
-----------

source-worktree
    `clean` means the worktree carried no uncommitted change when this package
    was configured; `dirty` means it did, and the source-revision above
    therefore does NOT fully identify the inputs. `unknown` means neither could
    be established — the source tree was not a git repository (a source-tarball
    build, for instance) and no explicit value was supplied.

    The value is normally measured with `git status --porcelain`. It can also be
    supplied at configure time via FIXPP_PACKAGE_WORKTREE, alongside
    FIXPP_PACKAGE_REVISION, for builds from a non-git source tree; in that case
    it is an assertion by the builder rather than a measurement, and is only as
    trustworthy as that build's inputs.

    Configuration + revision alone is insufficient to detect staleness: two
    packages built from the same commit either side of an uncommitted edit are
    indistinguishable under that pair, and an uncommitted edit is the normal
    state of a working branch. A `dirty` package is not reproducible from its
    revision and must not be published.

telemetry
    Every artifact this project ships is built with the OpenTelemetry
    integration ON. An `OFF` value here means the package came from a
    development-accelerator build that must never have been packaged, and the
    packaging::telemetry-provenance gate is expected to reject it.
